
             GENERAL NOTES ON THE AMIGA VERSION OF PYTHON 2.7.18

                     Original notes: Irmen de Jong
                     Updated for AmigaPython 2.7.18


REGULAR PYTHON DOCUMENTATION AND RESOURCES
------------------------------------------

This Amiga documentation assumes you are familiar with Python in general.
If not, the base documentation (tutorial, language reference guide and
library reference manual) for Python 2.7 can be obtained from
https://docs.python.org/2.7/.  This is the main Python documentation site.

Historic AmigaPython 2.0 docs and Aminet archives remain useful background
for Amiga-only behaviour; this tree documents the 2.7.18 port.


MODULE SEARCH PATH
------------------

sys.path construction follows modern Python 2.7 rules, adapted for Amiga
path syntax (semicolon-separated PYTHONPATH entries, Amiga assigns, etc.):

- Paths are derived from the executable location / PYTHONHOME when set.
- If the global environment variable PYTHONPATH is set, those directories
  are inserted.  PYTHONPATH is a semicolon (;) separated list, e.g.
  "WORK:pylib;Python:lib".
- The directory of the script given on the command line is inserted in
  front of the search path.  If no script was given, '' (current directory)
  is inserted.

site.py is imported by default when Python is initialized; use -S to
disable it.  site.py extends the path with site-packages under the versioned
library directory (lib/python2.7/), site-python under lib/, and pathnames
mentioned in *.pth files found in those directories.

For those who want to know everything, see Modules/getpath.c and the
official Python 2.7 docs on sys.path / PYTHONHOME / PYTHONPATH.


MODULE FILENAMES AND CASE SENSITIVITY
-------------------------------------

AmigaPython checks the case of the imported module against the case of
the module's filename.  Say you try to "import stringio", this will fail
if the file is named "StringIO.py": the case is different.  If you don't
like this behavior (because it breaks older code) you can disable it by
setting the local or global environment variable PYTHONCASEOK to some value.


AMIGA SPECIFIC MODULES
----------------------

Amiga-specific library files live under lib/site-python (and related site
paths).  site.py is imported by default (unless -S), so those modules are
normally importable without extra path fiddling.

You can import Amiga specific modules whether they are builtin (like parts
of ARexx support) or .py files:

	import ARexx

In 2.7.18, many Dos.library helpers, path converters, ASL/catalog/icon
APIs are also available directly on the builtin `amiga` module.  See
amiga_module and Dos_module.


POSIX REPLACEMENTS
------------------

In lib/ there is a dummy module `posixpath'.  It just imports `amigapath',
thereby making some other modules and files work because they don't use
os.path but posixpath.

The builtin `amiga' module fills the role of Unix `posix' for the `os'
package.  Prefer `import os` for portable code.


About the networking support (bsdsocket.library)
------------------------------------------------

The interpreter works without a TCP stack, but socket-related modules need
bsdsocket.library (AmiTCP historically; modern stacks such as Roadshow also
provide it).  Some extra functions already work if you only have
usergroup.library: crypt, pwd and grp for instance.

I-Net225 is not supported (dropped in late 1.5.x AmigaPython).


About Timezones
---------------

Timezone information is taken from locale preferences when available.
If locale prefs couldn't be read, timezone information is taken from
ENV:TZ, which must be a string like 'MET-1' (MET, -1 hour from GMT).
The timezone name string may be empty ('') depending on the C library.


sys.platform
------------

This is defined as 'amiga'. No upper case A.


os.uname
--------

The machine information identifies the Kickstart release version and
related hardware/OS details exposed by the port's uname implementation.


os.popen
--------

When opening a pipe for writing (like f=os.popen('command','w')), using the
standard 'More' command doesn't work.  This is a fault of More.  Use a
better pager like Most (available on Aminet).


About modifying library files
-----------------------------

You should not change the *behaviour* of standard library modules in ways
that break cross-platform expectations.  Put Amiga specific library files
in the right place: lib/site-python (or site-packages as appropriate).


Workbench startup
-----------------

Amiga Python contains code to start the interpreter and scripts from the
Workbench.

1. Just starting Python from the Workbench.
   Tooltypes are converted to command-line arguments.
   One tooltype is one argument. So to specify '-v -t -i' as command line
   arguments, you must have three tooltypes. One -v, one -t and one -i.

2. Multiselecting Python with a Python script.
   The script is executed. Tooltypes from the script's icon are appended to
   the arguments from the tooltypes from Python's icon.

3. Datafile with project icon normally used for Python scripts.
   See MAGIC TOOLTYPES / DATA FILES below.

SCRIPT ICONS.
Look in the `Icons' directory for icon templates: use def_py.info and
def_pyc.info as icon templates for .py and .pyc files.  Just make sure the
default tool is set to `Python:Python'.  Any tooltypes in the icon are
converted to command line arguments for the Python interpreter (one
tooltype is exactly one command line argument).

MAGIC TOOLTYPES.
Two 'magic tooltypes' control how icons and tooltypes are parsed:
'PYTHONSCRIPT=...' and 'PYSCRIPTARG=...'.

DATA FILES.
It is possible to start Python with a given script by doubleclicking on a
DATA FILE'S ICON.  Give the data file a Python script icon whose default
tool is Python:Python.  Add a tooltype PYTHONSCRIPT=... naming the script
to run.  The data file becomes an argument in sys.argv.

SCRIPT ARGUMENTS TOOLTYPES.
The magic tooltype 'PYSCRIPTARG=...' moves the '...' part to the end of the
argument list, after the script name, so it becomes a script parameter
rather than an interpreter option.  For a data file, the data file's
filename is placed *before* the script arguments.

NO-SCRIPT PYTHON SCRIPTS.
You can embed a tiny script in the icon alone: tooltype '-c' and the next
tooltype containing the code (without quotes), for example:

-c
import os,sys; print sys.argv; print os.uname()


